libcamera: software_isp: add radial lens-shading correction

A lens delivers less light to the corners of the image than to its centre,
and on the OnePlus 7 Pro main camera the corners get only 25-32% of it, about
two stops, with red falling off faster than green and blue, so they come out
dark and tinted. A flat-field fit gives a radial gain per channel,
k0 + k1*r^2 + k2*r^4 + k3*r^6, with r measured from the optical centre in
fractions of the image size, so r = 1 at the corners.

The new LensShading algorithm of the simple IPA reads that fit from the
sensor's tuning file and hands it to the debayer in DebayerParams. Its keys
are 'r', 'g' and 'b', the four coefficients of each channel and the only
required ones, 'centre' for the optical centre as fractions of the image
(default 0.5, 0.5), 'strength' as a 0..1 blend towards no correction
(default 1) and 'maxGain' as the clamp of the gains (default 4). The two GPU
fragment shaders apply the gain to the demosaiced linear signal before white
balance and colour correction, at the position the output pixel samples on
the sensor, so a stream scaled down from the sensor keeps the fit; a tuning
file without LensShading applies a gain of exactly 1. The CPU debayer folds
its processing into lookup tables on the pixel value and cannot carry a gain
that varies across the frame, so it logs a one-time warning if the
correction is tuned in.
---
